# Updating zwave-js-ui (Docker, DietPi RPi4)

Host: `rpi4-dietpi` · Container: `zwave-js-ui` · Image: `zwavejs/zwave-js-ui`

## Why this comes up

Home Assistant Core checks the **internal zwave-js-server protocol version**
("api schema") bundled inside zwave-js-ui — separate from the zwave-js-ui
app version number itself. A HA Core update can require a newer api schema
than your current zwave-js-ui build supports, breaking the Z-Wave JS
integration until the container is updated.

Typical errors seen in HA:
- `The version of Z-Wave Server you are currently running is too old for
  this version of Home Assistant.`
- `Invalid server version: Z-Wave JS Server version (x.x.x) is
  incompatible. Update the Z-Wave JS Server to a version that supports at
  least api schema NN.`

## Current container config (as of 2026-10)

```
Volume:  zwave-config:/usr/src/app/store
Device:  /dev/ttyACM0:/dev/ttyACM0
Ports:   3000:3000, 8091:8091
Restart: unless-stopped
```

## Steps

### 1. Back up the current container config (before touching anything)
```bash
sudo docker inspect zwave-js-ui > zwave-js-ui-backup-config.json
```
Keep this somewhere safe. If anything goes sideways, it has the exact
Binds/Devices/Ports to recreate from.

### 2. Stop and remove the old container
The `zwave-config` named volume persists independently of the container —
removing the container does **not** touch paired devices/network config.
```bash
sudo docker stop zwave-js-ui
sudo docker rm zwave-js-ui
```

### 3. Remove the stale local image — don't skip this
**Lesson learned (Oct 2026):** `docker pull zwavejs/zwave-js-ui:latest`
silently reused the cached local image instead of fetching a new one, even
though a newer version existed on the registry. Recreating the container
afterward just relaunched the same old version. Explicitly removing the
local image first forces a real re-pull.
```bash
sudo docker rmi zwavejs/zwave-js-ui:latest
sudo docker images zwavejs/zwave-js-ui   # should return nothing
```

### 4. Pull fresh and watch the output
```bash
sudo docker pull zwavejs/zwave-js-ui:latest
```
Confirm you see real `Downloading`/`Extracting` progress for multiple
layers (hundreds of MB), not an instant "already up to date." If it's
suspiciously fast, the pull likely didn't get anything new — check
https://hub.docker.com/r/zwavejs/zwave-js-ui/tags for the current version
and pull that explicit tag instead of `:latest`.

### 5. Recreate the container
```bash
sudo docker run -d \
  --name zwave-js-ui \
  --restart unless-stopped \
  -v zwave-config:/usr/src/app/store \
  --device=/dev/ttyACM0:/dev/ttyACM0 \
  -p 3000:3000 \
  -p 8091:8091 \
  zwavejs/zwave-js-ui:latest
```

### 6. Verify the version actually changed
```bash
sudo docker inspect zwave-js-ui --format '{{json .Config.Labels}}'
```
Check `org.opencontainers.image.version` and `...created` — both should be
newer than before the update, not identical.

### 7. Verify devices survived
Open `http://<rpi-ip>:8091` and confirm all paired Z-Wave devices are still
listed.

### 8. Verify Home Assistant is happy
Check Settings → Devices & services → Z-Wave JS integration — the error
should clear on its own retry, or restart HA Core if it doesn't within a
minute or two.

## If `:latest` still doesn't move after removing the local image

Docker Hub's `latest` tag can occasionally lag behind the project's actual
newest release. Pull an explicit version tag instead:
```bash
sudo docker pull zwavejs/zwave-js-ui:<version>
```
then recreate using that explicit tag in place of `:latest` in step 5.
